WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.602
Hey Arjun.

00:00:00.602 --> 00:00:01.102
Maxime.

00:00:01.102 --> 00:00:06.647
It's good to have you here. I guess as a first question, what brought you into Zcash?

00:00:06.647 --> 00:00:09.177
Well, I was interested in cryptocurrencies, specifically Bitcoin,

00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:13.920
for ideological reasons, right? I read Austrian economics and I was like,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.400
okay, the state shouldn't have control of the money supply and shouldn't arbitrarily,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.760
on a whim, be able to inflate the money supply, because what it basically does is reward

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.280
insiders and the people who are closest to the source of that new money generation,

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.600
money printing. And I was like, okay, but then what's the alternative,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.984
right? And then I just discovered Bitcoin around COVID, or a little after that,

00:00:39.984 --> 00:00:45.511
and I just went down the rabbit hole, and then Bitcoin just made sense,

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:48.560
right? It's this perfect alternative to fiat money.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.067
Sooner rather than later I realized it's not private, which is a huge flaw.

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:59.600
Essentially, if I'm making a bank transfer to you, it's more private than using Bitcoin,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.193
right? Or if I'm using my credit card, sure, I'm giving information to the credit card

00:01:04.193 --> 00:01:08.135
company and the state, but I'm not leaking it to everybody else in the world,

00:01:08.135 --> 00:01:14.320
right? And AI is just leading to all these data hacks, and data hacks are more and more

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.594
common, so eventually that data is also leaked.

00:01:16.594 --> 00:01:20.535
So the problem was just, if the data exists, it can be used against you.

00:01:20.535 --> 00:01:24.027
People think, oh, but I've not done anything wrong, why do I need privacy?

00:01:24.027 --> 00:01:27.760
People don't realize that that definition of legal is so broad,

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.792
they can pretty much find anything with any person and they can use it against you and

00:01:31.792 --> 00:01:33.436
be like, okay, this is wrong.

00:01:33.436 --> 00:01:39.000
So eventually I got into Zcash because a bunch of friends were talking about it.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.970
Started looking into it. I was like, okay, it's just Bitcoin:

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:47.093
perfect fair distribution, only 21 million ever, and it just adds encryption and zero-knowledge

00:01:47.093 --> 00:01:50.912
proofs to make it private. So I was like, this is just a better alternative.

00:01:50.912 --> 00:01:55.244
Then I heard about its plans to scale and go post-quantum, and I was like,

00:01:55.244 --> 00:01:59.666
okay, this just makes sense. Bitcoin's sort of ossifying and we have this alternative,

00:01:59.666 --> 00:02:04.120
and it really aims to be money. Bitcoin aimed to be money before,

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.069
but they kind of gave up on that, and now the meme is digital store of value or digital gold.

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:12.303
But what I really care about is just the future of money.

00:02:12.303 --> 00:02:17.605
Right. So you're more into the idea of Zcash becoming actual electronic cash as opposed

00:02:17.605 --> 00:02:24.562
to becoming a store of value. I mean, I guess those are all basically also linked.

00:02:24.562 --> 00:02:27.580
Right, they don't have to be two separate things, right?

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.680
People think those are two separate things because fiat money is just inflated at a whim.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.500
It's not really at a whim, but the governments can do it when they have to pay off debt.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.980
But you can have a store of value that also serves as a medium of exchange long-term.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:47.905
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Coming back to the privacy part, where does AI fit into that?

00:02:47.905 --> 00:02:50.320
Yeah, AI is just accelerating surveillance, right?

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.060
So obviously I'm a huge fan of AI, I use it every day.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.189
It's an amazing tool, but it's in the hands of everybody, which is great,

00:02:57.189 --> 00:03:02.636
but it's nation states. Nation states are already using it to just automate surveillance,

00:03:02.636 --> 00:03:08.300
right? Earlier you had to use manual labor to go through heaps of data,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.198
and there would literally be a person going through all this data and trying to figure

00:03:12.198 --> 00:03:17.300
out how they can catch you. But now you just have AI just flagging transactions,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:23.682
Flock cameras in the US, right? So I think AI just accelerates the need for privacy in

00:03:23.682 --> 00:03:27.200
financial transactions and privacy everywhere else as well.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.596
I agree with that. I think what we've also started to notice with Bitcoin,

00:03:32.596 --> 00:03:37.029
for example, is you have these platforms like Arkham, for example,

00:03:37.029 --> 00:03:42.670
which make it available to anyone to trace you, and anyone, however badly intentioned,

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:46.700
to surveil people and to track every movement of Bitcoin.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:52.387
I think it's Ray Dalio who said that as a result, central banks are never actually going

00:03:52.387 --> 00:03:56.402
to adopt Bitcoin, because for geopolitical reasons they obviously cannot allow others

00:03:56.402 --> 00:04:02.380
to see their moves. And so it doesn't make sense to me that Zcash would fit in as both

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:07.140
a very good store of value, but also as a very good peer-to-peer cash mechanism.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.420
How do you think we're going to get to Zcash becoming money?

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:14.168
Because it's one thing to be an asset that people buy and sell,

00:04:14.168 --> 00:04:19.060
sort of like they've done with crypto and Bitcoin specifically for a very long time now.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:24.420
It's another to actually go and get the network effects required to be used in practice.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.780
Where do you see that going?

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.740
Yeah, I think it starts with being a store of value, right?

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.400
You become money by first becoming something that people don't want to give out very easily.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.167
People just want to hold on to it. And the network effect of money is such that,

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:42.016
before money, you had barter. So if I wanted shoes, if you're a shoemaker,

00:04:42.016 --> 00:04:45.780
I have apples, I want to trade my apples for your shoes.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.414
Maybe you don't want apples. So I look for this other alternative that serves as this

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:54.370
medium of exchange, and then eventually that alternative becomes common because it has

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:59.879
certain properties. So Zcash, I think it does have the strongest properties of money,

00:04:59.879 --> 00:05:04.500
at least for the sovereign individual type of money that I'm interested in.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.100
It might not be the easiest to use, but that's getting fixed.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.544
Actually, I would argue it's easier than banks, right?

00:05:09.544 --> 00:05:13.222
It's just people need to access it. Banks is so frustrating,

00:05:13.222 --> 00:05:17.393
man. You just have money stuck there for days and weeks, and people don't even care.

00:05:17.393 --> 00:05:18.980
People just take that for granted, right?

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.277
It's like, okay, this is how the banking system works.

00:05:21.277 --> 00:05:25.020
Yeah, that's your money, but the bank now owns it, and it's not actually your money.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.807
So Zcash is already easier to use than banks, right?

00:05:27.807 --> 00:05:30.348
If I download advisor and I just send it to your shoe without us,

00:05:30.348 --> 00:05:33.043
you receive it in seconds and you're able to spend it as well.

00:05:33.043 --> 00:05:37.678
And you can already use it as money today, where if people want USDC or people on Bitcoin,

00:05:37.678 --> 00:05:40.122
you can just pay them in whatever currency they desire.

00:05:40.122 --> 00:05:44.688
A lot of people I know are already using it as money.

00:05:44.688 --> 00:05:48.742
To get to everybody accepting Zcash, it really becoming and replacing the US dollar,

00:05:48.742 --> 00:05:54.839
right, which is in my mind the goal. Being top three cryptocurrencies or replacing Bitcoin

00:05:54.839 --> 00:06:00.140
at the top, that's not really inspiring to me than just becoming money and then becoming

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:02.247
the free medium of exchange that people can use.

00:06:02.247 --> 00:06:05.960
Because there's so many things that depend on money, because it's a substrate for civilization

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.785
itself, right? It's a coordination tool, and if that base layer is not free,

00:06:10.785 --> 00:06:13.707
it affects the higher-level layers, like science, philosophy,

00:06:13.707 --> 00:06:17.829
morality, right? So many things just depend on money, because governments can fund wars

00:06:17.829 --> 00:06:21.319
by just printing money. Once you kind of fix money at the base layer,

00:06:21.319 --> 00:06:24.911
there's a lot of things at the top that also accelerate and become better.

00:06:24.911 --> 00:06:28.687
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Hayek really talks about this in a very good way.

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:32.521
I think the way to rephrase it is that money is intertwined with every decision you make,

00:06:32.521 --> 00:06:36.250
whether, as I said, whether it's morality, whatever it is, there's always an economic

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:43.000
end to it. And then that base layer right now is both captured at the supply level,

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:49.640
where you're completely in the hands of the government, but also it's captured at the

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.040
privacy level, where you're getting surveilled.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:56.888
Bitcoin was sort of the proof of concept, so to say, to prove the fact that you could

00:06:56.888 --> 00:06:58.999
get a fixed supply, decentralization and stuff.

00:06:58.999 --> 00:07:06.040
But Bitcoin walked so that Zcash could run, and Zcash for the final evolution of Bitcoin there.

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:10.556
The banking system is just so bad. You make a transaction, it takes five days,

00:07:10.556 --> 00:07:13.520
they rip you off on the fees, on the yields.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.000
It's quite impressive that crypto is already a better UX, especially with passkeys and stuff.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.331
Yeah. If you try to withdraw a bunch of cash, they'll ask you all sorts of questions,

00:07:20.331 --> 00:07:24.580
even if it's your money you deposited. It's terrible, right?

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.580
Yeah. It's your property and they're making it difficult.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.740
You mentioned the sovereign individual. Where does that fit into the Zcash thesis?

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.680
I mean, it's obviously very aligned, but yeah.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:36.288
Yeah, I think the sovereign individual thesis is the Zcash thesis,

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:43.200
at least for me. You want to own your money yourself and you want to hold it privately

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.420
and transact privately. My transaction is none of anybody else's business,

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:51.926
unless I want to disclose it to them. People are traveling to other countries just to

00:07:51.926 --> 00:07:55.540
take therapies because that's banned in their country.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.420
So they're like, okay, going to Honduras, for example, and getting full-time gene therapy

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:04.135
or something like that. And then there's so many things that are just never funded because

00:08:04.135 --> 00:08:09.560
people are afraid of what other people will say, or it's just looked down upon.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.320
So many dissident ideas, right?

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.320
Even in science, you'll see they would not share their ideas with the general public

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:19.760
for a long time. They just go through those ideas in their head.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:23.820
Even Charles Darwin, he didn't publish his discovery until he realized somebody else

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:28.212
came up with it at the same time, and he was like, "Okay, now I've got to publish." So

00:08:28.212 --> 00:08:30.980
now it's like, I'm kind of more free to do it.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.040
Creativity also begins with privacy, right?

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:36.385
So if every action is watched or every financial transaction is watched,

00:08:36.385 --> 00:08:40.200
you're just going to be super hyper-aware and you're not going to be your true self.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.344
Yeah, it's actually impressive how a lot of the discoveries in science,

00:08:44.344 --> 00:08:52.186
for example, in the Middle Ages and the Renaissance, were discoveries that were so exceptionally

00:08:52.186 --> 00:08:56.887
controversial that the discoverers waited, in Darwin's case,

00:08:56.887 --> 00:09:02.827
I think it's like 20 years. In Copernicus's case, I think it was also something like

00:09:02.827 --> 00:09:06.138
20, 30 years, and he only published them on his deathbed, actually,

00:09:06.138 --> 00:09:09.473
as far as I know. Newton had something like this as well.

00:09:09.473 --> 00:09:15.016
We benefit daily from the fact that these people were able to publish their work in the end.

00:09:15.016 --> 00:09:20.546
Today, there are a ton of people that are not able to do so because of fear of repercussion.

00:09:20.546 --> 00:09:24.020
So I guess that brings us really to the argument of privacy.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:27.540
How do you relate, then, the need for privacy to morality?

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.615
How does that enable greater morality?

00:09:29.615 --> 00:09:35.175
Yeah, just on your thread earlier, you said there's so many people that were controversial

00:09:35.175 --> 00:09:38.015
then, but now their idea is a consensus.

00:09:38.015 --> 00:09:43.017
And then how many people exist today where they're too afraid to share their ideas,

00:09:43.017 --> 00:09:46.220
but they're too controversial? They're going to be consensus for tomorrow,

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:51.780
right? And we should never assume that we are infallible and we've reached this perfect

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:57.012
knowledge in all areas. It's a very optimistic stance to think we can be wrong about

00:09:57.012 --> 00:10:01.000
everything and we can make progress, and we can be even better than what we are currently,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.420
even though we live in the greatest of times, right?

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.089
Yes, but so many things can be even better.

00:10:06.089 --> 00:10:09.958
So I guess that's how it kind of ties into morality, where you kind of start with,

00:10:09.958 --> 00:10:13.940
okay, we're fallible. The only way to make progress is by correcting errors,

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.890
rightly. So it's not taken immediately, right?

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:22.542
People don't immediately accept the new ideas, because you have good reason to believe

00:10:22.542 --> 00:10:26.782
the previous ideas, there's some truth in them, because it's worked so far.

00:10:26.782 --> 00:10:31.807
There's good rules of thumb. You could be making so much more progress on the margin.

00:10:31.807 --> 00:10:34.980
Privacy, I think, just enables those discoveries to happen.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.447
America was founded on privacy, right? The Founding Fathers,

00:10:38.447 --> 00:10:42.660
they just wrote anonymous letters. So that's how new discoveries are made.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:47.300
You want the person to be abstracted away from the ideas, and people to just judge the ideas.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:52.136
Yeah, that makes sense. It's actually crazy how bad we would be doing if it were not

00:10:52.136 --> 00:10:56.034
for privacy. Coming back, then, to the Founding Fathers, as you said,

00:10:56.034 --> 00:10:58.340
it's literally baked into the United States.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:02.233
It's the Bill of Rights, it's free speech, it's all these things.

00:11:02.233 --> 00:11:06.847
It's a core element of freedom. As you said, you can't have this free exploration of

00:11:06.847 --> 00:11:10.409
ideas that are sometimes going to be very, very controversial without that privacy.

00:11:10.409 --> 00:11:14.521
And we see it every day with these topics, as I said, in bio,

00:11:14.521 --> 00:11:18.957
and I don't know, IQ and all these things that are very, very controversial,

00:11:18.957 --> 00:11:27.070
and where there's a fundamental lack of progress due to how taboo and how controversial

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:31.150
those subjects are, which is really quite sad.

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:36.682
Circling back, then, to morality, how do you feel that comes into play here?

00:11:36.682 --> 00:11:39.766
My assumption has always been that greater wealth enables greater morality,

00:11:39.766 --> 00:11:44.020
because you have more choices, and so you have more freedom to do,

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:45.420
you know, the right thing, quote unquote.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.620
How do you feel about that?

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:50.692
Yeah, I think David Deutsch's definition of wealth is it's a repertoire of physical transformations

00:11:50.692 --> 00:11:56.057
we can cause, right? But morality constrains those physical transformations,

00:11:56.057 --> 00:11:59.418
because there are potentially infinite transformations we can cause,

00:11:59.418 --> 00:12:03.100
but we're not going to cause all of them, because our time is limited.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:09.460
And even if it was infinite, right, we would still have the problem of what to do next.

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:15.351
And that's, I think, the role of morality in that: what action should I take next that

00:12:15.351 --> 00:12:19.620
is correct? So I think wealth and morality are deeply connected.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:21.900
You just have infinite choices in front of you.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.167
What are you gonna choose?

00:12:23.167 --> 00:12:27.616
Yeah. Coming back to Zcash now, where do you see this going in,

00:12:27.616 --> 00:12:30.985
I guess, five, ten years? What's the vision down the line?

00:12:30.985 --> 00:12:35.711
Yeah, it's hard to predict the growth of knowledge, but it's different if you're trying

00:12:35.711 --> 00:12:39.498
to make it happen. So I think my goal, and people have talked to,

00:12:39.498 --> 00:12:43.855
the end state of Zcash and of crypto and money is just be unstoppable private money.

00:12:43.855 --> 00:12:49.522
So if we are the currency that more than a billion people use to transact in,

00:12:49.522 --> 00:12:54.193
that for me would be a win, not replacing Bitcoin.

00:12:54.193 --> 00:13:00.146
Those are just wins along the way, but the end state would be just becoming money.

00:13:00.146 --> 00:13:05.860
Yeah, yeah, yeah, right. And I think the beautiful part of Zcash is you don't even need

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:12.160
to necessarily become the number one currency to be able to already be usable,

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:15.502
compared to, for example, the US dollar, where you need basically everyone to be using

00:13:15.502 --> 00:13:18.116
the US dollar, or other currencies like USDC or whatever.

00:13:18.116 --> 00:13:23.570
With Zcash, with the ability to swap for other currencies, it's already usable today.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:26.280
You just need one cryptocurrency to be used.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.748
Where you want to actually be purchasing something or buying something,

00:13:29.748 --> 00:13:33.587
you just swap Zcash for it and you're good to go.

00:13:33.587 --> 00:13:38.622
So it's this really versatile currency with this isomorphism where you can swap any currency

00:13:38.622 --> 00:13:42.240
for another. It feels a lot like, obviously, way better if we get there.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.678
There's no need for Zcash to become the number one currency.

00:13:45.678 --> 00:13:47.400
It already works great now.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:52.411
Yeah. You do want people to accept Zcash just because you do leak privacy when you go

00:13:52.411 --> 00:13:56.280
to USDC, right? So that still exists. But you can already use it as money today,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:01.055
which is pretty awesome. And every time you kind of pay somebody in ZEC,

00:14:01.055 --> 00:14:04.857
they can't see it, right? So maybe they go on the block explorer and they're like,

00:14:04.857 --> 00:14:08.036
where'd this money come from? And so they learn about Zcash that way,

00:14:08.036 --> 00:14:13.286
right? And they're like, okay, maybe next time you pay me, I'll accept Zcash.

00:14:13.286 --> 00:14:17.979
Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly. The roadmap for Zcash, at least in my eyes,

00:14:17.979 --> 00:14:22.026
right now is pretty clear. It's Tachyon, quantum resistance,

00:14:22.026 --> 00:14:26.016
and just speeding up the whole chain with Zakura and other initiatives.

00:14:26.016 --> 00:14:30.006
What's your view of the Zcash roadmap for the next couple of years?

00:14:30.006 --> 00:14:34.324
Yeah, I think the important things are going post-quantum, since that seems to be a threat,

00:14:34.324 --> 00:14:39.340
right? So going fully post-quantum, not just having a quantum-recoverable pool.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:43.000
You just want every address, every transaction to be post-quantum.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.987
And scale is important, right? If you want to have a billion people using it,

00:14:45.987 --> 00:14:50.293
you need to be able to have the infrastructure for it, and right now no cryptocurrency

00:14:50.293 --> 00:14:55.770
really does. If a billion people try to use Bitcoin, it wouldn't work.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:01.184
We should dramatically improve TPS, just exponentially, and then also make the UX,

00:15:01.184 --> 00:15:05.592
it's already pretty good, but just far easier, right?

00:15:05.592 --> 00:15:10.853
You really want it to feel like you're using a card at a grocery store or something.

00:15:10.853 --> 00:15:16.187
Yeah, yeah, that makes sense. How do you feel about the transparent pool in Zcash?

00:15:16.187 --> 00:15:19.838
Do you think that we should keep it? Should it go away?

00:15:19.838 --> 00:15:20.859
Where's your head?

00:15:20.859 --> 00:15:24.653
I think I keep going back and forth on this, but I think it's fine for optionality.

00:15:24.653 --> 00:15:29.160
I know you have a viewing key with the shielded pool, but right now,

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:33.627
just for access, it just allows for the integrations, right?

00:15:33.627 --> 00:15:38.175
If you want to use NEAR Intents, you kind of want NEAR's address to be public.

00:15:38.175 --> 00:15:43.468
They could use the viewing key, again, but the world's heading into this whole surveillance thing.

00:15:43.468 --> 00:15:49.916
It's so normalized that's hard to break in unless you have sort of this layer that lets

00:15:49.916 --> 00:15:52.596
you kind of integrate with them easier, right?

00:15:52.596 --> 00:15:57.130
And you do want to integrate with these traditional people so you can onboard more people

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:59.729
and eventually just make the shielded pool normal, right?

00:15:59.729 --> 00:16:01.988
The shielded pool has grown exponentially also.

00:16:01.988 --> 00:16:05.876
Hopefully it just keeps growing and we go to like 90 or 100.

00:16:05.876 --> 00:16:09.728
Yeah, that makes sense. How do you feel about, for example, the dev fund?

00:16:09.728 --> 00:16:11.421
Where's your head on that subject?

00:16:11.421 --> 00:16:15.647
Yeah, I mean, you had a great article on it, which sparked a lot of debates,

00:16:15.647 --> 00:16:20.920
so props to you on that. I'm mostly with you that we should discontinue the dev fund entirely.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:26.653
I don't think we need to be super urgent about it, although the conversation is,

00:16:26.653 --> 00:16:28.840
I think it's the right time to have a conversation.

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:31.851
It automatically expires in two years, I believe, right?

00:16:31.851 --> 00:16:32.520
And so—

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:33.800
The inflows end in two years.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:39.480
Right. We could just let it expire. Just do nothing, let it expire.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.520
Because it's been, I think it's just been two cycles where it's been renewed,

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:46.520
two or three. And this time we could just, we've started the project,

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:48.746
even though private donors have funded it as well.

00:16:48.746 --> 00:16:51.960
And all the arguments you made in your post were pretty good,

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:55.038
although I disagree with calling it a tax, since it's a voluntary system.

00:16:55.038 --> 00:16:57.463
I won't head back. I agree with that.

00:16:57.463 --> 00:17:02.152
Yeah, yeah, yeah, that makes sense. But maybe explain your current understanding,

00:17:02.152 --> 00:17:06.288
and after all the discussions of the community, where do you stand now?

00:17:06.288 --> 00:17:11.871
I think I'm still pretty convinced by my position, but I think there's a rough consensus

00:17:11.871 --> 00:17:14.581
around the fact that retroactive grants should stay.

00:17:14.581 --> 00:17:20.868
And I feel like retroactive grants are probably the least bad part of the dev fund,

00:17:20.868 --> 00:17:24.900
just because it's retroactive. You can see the work beforehand.

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:27.260
It's coinholder-voted. I guess I'm okay with it.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:32.178
Where I feel more strongly is ZCG, just because they have so much funding,

00:17:32.178 --> 00:17:38.593
coinholders are not represented. In February this year, there was a coinholder vote against

00:17:38.593 --> 00:17:43.000
ZSAs, and ZSAs were rejected with, I think it was like 99%.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:49.017
Voters voted 99% against something, and then ZCG is, with their money,

00:17:49.017 --> 00:17:53.840
still allocating capital to ZSAs. There's clearly a problem there.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.834
And then when you add in the fact that ZCG is elected by ZCAP,

00:17:57.834 --> 00:18:01.448
ZCAP, which you can join by being active on the forum, for example,

00:18:01.448 --> 00:18:01.948
or—

00:18:01.948 --> 00:18:04.040
Or receiving a grant from ZCG.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:08.480
Or receiving a grant from ZCG. These mechanisms are really, really terrible.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:12.351
Obviously, if we let this continue, things are going to go very bad.

00:18:12.351 --> 00:18:16.962
Either someone is going to Sybil attack ZCAP and just join with a ton of accounts and

00:18:16.962 --> 00:18:20.806
then vote in the next people on ZCG and steal a lot of money,

00:18:20.806 --> 00:18:22.660
or something else is going to happen, I don't know.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:27.396
But you have to assume that two, three years down the line, when ZEC is hopefully at

00:18:27.396 --> 00:18:31.300
a much higher price, this is going to attract a lot of nefarious people.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:34.480
And so I think it would be good to lock it down now.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.900
I would be fine with even just making it coinholder-accountable.

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:43.623
Just make the elections have coinholder voting, and then coinholders can decide,

00:18:43.623 --> 00:18:46.420
because in the end, it's their money. Their money is being spent.

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:47.420
They should have the choice.

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:50.420
Yeah. I think I agree.

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:54.140
Yeah. So let's then circle back to Deutsch, and to David Deutsch's work.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.780
How do you tie his work into Zcash?

00:18:56.780 --> 00:19:01.380
I think there was a tweet from him saying that privacy is part of creativity.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:06.800
So as I was talking about, you can't have freedom or creativity without privacy.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:10.346
Any new thought that you have exists in your mind.

00:19:10.346 --> 00:19:14.439
Nobody else can access it immediately, and then you choose to put it on paper and to

00:19:14.439 --> 00:19:19.242
share it with other people. So yeah, I don't know if, this is obviously not putting words

00:19:19.242 --> 00:19:21.720
into David's mouth. He's never talked about Zcash.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:26.207
He just replied to one Tachyon tweet, just making a funny joke about it.

00:19:26.207 --> 00:19:29.736
I don't remember the joke, but he was just like, I don't know if it'll ever arrive,

00:19:29.736 --> 00:19:33.600
or something related to Tachyon. There's so many aspects to this work.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.700
The main thing that I've learned is just be optimistic, and problems are soluble.

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:41.060
You always want the good people fighting against the enemies of civilization,

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:43.760
and I think Zcash is a good tool to do that.

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:48.995
Yeah, that makes sense. I think it's really at the core of the Popperian philosophy of

00:19:48.995 --> 00:19:53.815
conjecture, refutation. If you know that this conjecture will lead you to be persecuted

00:19:53.815 --> 00:19:57.548
or attacked, well, you're simply going to self-censor and never make that conjecture

00:19:57.548 --> 00:20:00.840
in the first place, and that really limits progress as a whole.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.012
You never see the unseen, like the things that never happen,

00:20:04.012 --> 00:20:04.512
right?

00:20:04.512 --> 00:20:05.012
Right.

00:20:05.012 --> 00:20:06.400
You never know about the unseen things.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:11.018
Yeah, that makes sense. How do you see Zcash growing in the next few years,

00:20:11.018 --> 00:20:14.949
right? People have tried through various ways to grow Zcash,

00:20:14.949 --> 00:20:19.303
focus on certain audiences and stuff. Who do you think is the target audience right now

00:20:19.303 --> 00:20:20.080
for Zcash?

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:23.404
I think right now, if you're long AI, you're already long Zcash,

00:20:23.404 --> 00:20:28.188
right? It's AI-resistant money. But also, if you think the world's headed into this communist

00:20:28.188 --> 00:20:32.765
state type thing, especially in America and Europe, you're already seeing all these taxes

00:20:32.765 --> 00:20:37.580
being levied, or capital controls. You're just going to want some insurance against that.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:41.405
Yeah, yeah. The term that I really like for Zcash is communism insurance,

00:20:41.405 --> 00:20:42.200
right?

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:45.400
So the people who want communism insurance, Zcash is for you.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:48.260
But more generally, I think it's for everybody, right?

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:53.180
Because money is for everybody. And then you want people to own their own money and use

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:56.920
it however they like, as long as they're not hurting anybody.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:21:01.521
And there's ways to cash that. You don't need any transaction to be public.

00:21:01.521 --> 00:21:05.711
I think first it would really appeal to the freedom-loving crowd,

00:21:05.711 --> 00:21:10.960
maybe the people in America who own guns, people like gold, just their own,

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:16.154
really being able to own yourself. And eventually that sort of hopefully spreads to everybody else.

00:21:16.154 --> 00:21:21.260
That makes sense. Coming back to Bitcoin, I think one of the big problems with Bitcoin

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:25.260
in the last few years has been capture, right?

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:28.833
There's essentially been this institutional capture of Bitcoin,

00:21:28.833 --> 00:21:34.282
where it lost a lot of its ethos and, frankly, became uninteresting.

00:21:34.282 --> 00:21:39.570
How do we avoid this? How do we make sure that that doesn't happen with Zcash?

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:43.920
Obviously, there were Bitcoiners, and I think there still are,

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:48.662
who really cared about the cypherpunk ideals, and that's the only reason they were in

00:21:48.662 --> 00:21:49.208
it for.

00:21:49.208 --> 00:21:53.887
The thing about regulatory capture is, since it is a free market,

00:21:53.887 --> 00:21:57.787
anybody can really come in. There's nobody stopping Saylor from buying coins.

00:21:57.787 --> 00:22:01.100
From Saylor, that could even happen to Zcash, right?

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:05.104
You can have a Saylor for Zcash. People just come in and buy and try to regulate these things.

00:22:05.104 --> 00:22:08.520
They're already seeing ETFs, which I think is fine.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:13.479
But as long as you have a base group of people, the core group,

00:22:13.479 --> 00:22:18.229
the core developers, engineers, and the core community, cares about freedom,

00:22:18.229 --> 00:22:22.282
does not want to, you're talking about removing transparent,

00:22:22.282 --> 00:22:26.478
right? We could also remove shielded. That's technically possible,

00:22:26.478 --> 00:22:33.029
but it's never going to happen. And Bitcoiners take pride in being trackable.

00:22:33.029 --> 00:22:37.020
If you go to the Bitcoin Twitter page, it does say trackable digital gold or something.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:38.300
Oh wow, that's crazy.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:44.945
Yeah. So if you have this core community that really, the project really exists because

00:22:44.945 --> 00:22:48.827
there was this giant flaw in Bitcoin, I don't really see shielded ever disappearing,

00:22:48.827 --> 00:22:52.852
right? So as long as we have this freedom crowd that really believes in the project and

00:22:52.852 --> 00:22:56.471
is behind the project. I think the coinholders, I don't know who the coinholders are,

00:22:56.471 --> 00:23:00.129
right? There's so many, but a few of my friends I've talked to,

00:23:00.129 --> 00:23:05.344
they really care about this. But there could be others; who knows what they're in it for.

00:23:05.344 --> 00:23:08.991
That makes sense. I think at a certain level it's really a cultural problem.

00:23:08.991 --> 00:23:14.220
The way it happened with Bitcoin is you got all these civil wars in Bitcoin in the early

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:19.726
days, and then a lot of the early adopters got rich, and then they stopped caring.

00:23:19.726 --> 00:23:25.380
I think one of the really nice things about Zcash is that if it succeeds,

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.503
it doesn't actually even matter whether there's institutionalization,

00:23:28.503 --> 00:23:31.605
because the shielded pool will be there.

00:23:31.605 --> 00:23:37.485
And that will probably ensure that in the long run, these institutions like BlackRock

00:23:37.485 --> 00:23:41.692
and stuff will probably stay away from Zcash, just due to the regulatory pressure.

00:23:41.692 --> 00:23:46.576
And I guess that leads me to the subject of how do you feel governments are going to

00:23:46.576 --> 00:23:50.620
view Zcash and interact with it in the next few years?

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:55.260
There's been the war on crypto the past few years.

00:23:55.260 --> 00:24:01.500
Are we going to go to a war on privacy if we get President AOC tomorrow?

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:06.426
I'm sure there will be, in a way that will increase the demand for Zcash,

00:24:06.426 --> 00:24:08.340
because, again, it's communism insurance.

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:13.777
Yeah, I definitely foresee that happening with all governments,

00:24:13.777 --> 00:24:18.380
not just America. Maybe Milei is an exception, but even there,

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:21.664
the incentives are just in a way that you want to surveil people,

00:24:21.664 --> 00:24:25.395
right? You want to know what's happening so you can control them.

00:24:25.395 --> 00:24:29.870
That's definitely something we should be thinking about, and that's really why it's important

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:32.136
to onboard as many people now as possible, right?

00:24:32.136 --> 00:24:35.584
If you have a billion people using this, and if it's normal,

00:24:35.584 --> 00:24:38.475
you can't shut it down. Sure, you can delist it off of exchanges,

00:24:38.475 --> 00:24:42.100
but a billion holders, right? I'll just buy it from you or somebody else.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:50.481
So that's why we should just be doing everything we can to sort of memetically onboard

00:24:50.481 --> 00:24:52.044
as many people as possible.

00:24:52.044 --> 00:24:56.652
The current administration seems kind of okay in the US, at least,

00:24:56.652 --> 00:25:00.102
because the president's launching meme coins, which is awesome.

00:25:00.102 --> 00:25:05.642
But that could change very soon. And right now we're also pretty small for them to really care.

00:25:05.642 --> 00:25:11.702
We've already grown quite a lot since the last two years, and I expect that to be happening.

00:25:11.702 --> 00:25:16.790
And so once you reach sort of the threshold and people start noticing you,

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:20.234
that's really when you just want to be unstoppable, right?

00:25:20.234 --> 00:25:25.668
Yeah, that makes sense. As you said, the government's cracking down on privacy demonstrates

00:25:25.668 --> 00:25:29.548
the need for privacy. It's quite ironic.

00:25:29.548 --> 00:25:35.514
Speaking of Bitcoin, where do you see Bitcoin going, then, with Zcash doing so well,

00:25:35.514 --> 00:25:38.567
having selected privacy narrative, having quantum resistance,

00:25:38.567 --> 00:25:43.142
having the scalability? What is Bitcoin becoming in that world?

00:25:43.142 --> 00:25:49.152
I don't really know. You had that shielded Bitcoin paper recently that a lot of people

00:25:49.152 --> 00:25:53.441
were talking about, and a lot of people asked me about it, and I was pretty sure that's

00:25:53.441 --> 00:25:58.140
never going to happen. If it ever happens, it's probably going to be after Zcash passes

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:01.505
Bitcoin and they realize, oh, we kind of need privacy.

00:26:01.505 --> 00:26:05.272
But you've already seen them kind of struggle with post-quantum plans,

00:26:05.272 --> 00:26:07.756
and really a path to being post-quantum.

00:26:07.756 --> 00:26:11.807
As far as I know, there's not really any plans to scale.

00:26:11.807 --> 00:26:15.435
They're fine just being at 3 TPS or whatever they are right now,

00:26:15.435 --> 00:26:18.117
10 TPS maybe. It can't be money that way.

00:26:18.117 --> 00:26:21.428
Just in that sense, Zcash is already a superior money, right?

00:26:21.428 --> 00:26:26.033
But maybe just trying to be like gold. But it's also not gold,

00:26:26.033 --> 00:26:30.999
because gold's private. Although you have the problem with transporting gold across physical distances.

00:26:30.999 --> 00:26:34.452
You can't really do that, because if you try to take it to an airport,

00:26:34.452 --> 00:26:36.772
they'll be like, okay, gotta go through customs and stuff.

00:26:36.772 --> 00:26:40.780
But with Zcash, you can just use it as digital gold as well as money.

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:43.925
So I don't really know where Bitcoin goes from here.

00:26:43.925 --> 00:26:48.700
Yeah, and you can see it with a lot of Bitcoiners getting really upset the Zcash price

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:50.884
is going up, and everybody's just calling it a pump and dump.

00:26:50.884 --> 00:26:54.513
And they've been calling it a pump and dump since it was like 80,

00:26:54.513 --> 00:26:58.719
right? I'm like, when will it stop, guys?

00:26:58.719 --> 00:27:03.260
Yeah, maybe there's a certain threshold where, and there probably will be a certain threshold,

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:06.826
but they do get in, and then it's just e-cash from there.

00:27:06.826 --> 00:27:10.163
Yeah, we can stay solvent longer than they can cope.

00:27:10.163 --> 00:27:14.184
I guess then, coming back to gold, do you have any thoughts about the future of gold?

00:27:14.184 --> 00:27:19.402
People have sort of laughed at me in the past for saying that gold is basically going

00:27:19.402 --> 00:27:22.558
to be very soon irrelevant with mining asteroids and stuff.

00:27:22.558 --> 00:27:25.361
Do you think that this is where we're going?

00:27:25.361 --> 00:27:27.929
Is anybody planning to mine an asteroid anytime soon?

00:27:27.929 --> 00:27:31.340
I mean, I think there's at least one company that I know of.

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:33.780
I think they're AstroForge, I think they're called.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.940
Eventually, Elon is just going to send rockets to Astro.

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:37.940
Yeah, eventually. It probably does happen.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.940
Yeah. And then it's like—

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:41.440
What replaces gold then?

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:42.940
Yeah. Might actually be Zcash.

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:46.576
Might actually be Zcash, because it's just intergalactic scalable money,

00:27:46.576 --> 00:27:49.460
right? You can just keep adding words to it.

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:50.460
Thermodynamic.

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:54.889
Yeah, yeah, yeah. But if that happens, then same thing that happened to cowrie shells

00:27:54.889 --> 00:27:59.320
back in the day, the supply getting inflated because somebody brought it from a different

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:00.840
island, that would probably happen to gold as well.

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.507
Yeah. I'm super optimistic about space exploration, right?

00:28:03.507 --> 00:28:08.420
These things, just keeping on making progress as a species and ascending to the next

00:28:08.420 --> 00:28:13.294
thing, I think it's important. And a lot of people kind of become pessimistic about AI

00:28:13.294 --> 00:28:15.400
taking over and AI doing everything else.

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:18.030
I think there will always be a special role for humans.

00:28:18.030 --> 00:28:20.980
We might become something else, I'm fine with that, but we're just people at the end

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:24.920
of the day, right? And we can create knowledge about anything that exists.

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:28.139
So yeah, I'm super optimistic, and it's a great time to be alive,

00:28:28.139 --> 00:28:32.627
and Zcash could be a little part of that future, of the grand future that we're dreaming about.

00:28:32.627 --> 00:28:37.920
Yeah, yeah, yeah, that makes sense. What's one thing that's currently overlooked or underrated

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:43.122
or missed in Zcash that you feel should have more attention?

00:28:43.122 --> 00:28:47.480
I think Tachyon definitely gets a lot of hype, just as it should,

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:51.901
although what Sean Bowe's team and Dev's team were doing with Zakura,

00:28:51.901 --> 00:28:56.200
the new Zcash node, is also really exponentially speeding things up,

00:28:56.200 --> 00:29:00.720
right? So the user experience is already way better.

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.120
Highly recommend people check out Zakura.

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:08.440
They've been posting super technical posts on their X page, which I can't say I understand

00:29:08.440 --> 00:29:11.775
all of, but I think what's happening with Zakura is important.

00:29:11.775 --> 00:29:15.598
You just need another node to, it's also good, decentralize it.

00:29:15.598 --> 00:29:19.187
What you guys are doing at Cypherpunk with mining is pretty good too.

00:29:19.187 --> 00:29:21.302
I think you're pointing at Zakura nodes?

00:29:21.302 --> 00:29:23.418
Yes. Yeah, yeah, so we're running Zakura.

00:29:23.418 --> 00:29:25.386
Yeah, let's go, let's kill the chain.

00:29:25.386 --> 00:29:30.742
Just one more question. With regards to, Bitcoin has sort of had this ethos of,

00:29:30.742 --> 00:29:34.360
let's sort of stop progress, let's be very, very conservative.

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:37.991
Obviously Tachyon is a very, very, very ambitious scaling plan.

00:29:37.991 --> 00:29:41.888
It really completely rethinks the way we think of blockchains.

00:29:41.888 --> 00:29:46.916
Same for Zakura on the node side. Do you feel like there will be a point where,

00:29:46.916 --> 00:29:50.783
I guess I'm sort of interested in knowing when you feel that would be the case,

00:29:50.783 --> 00:29:54.996
we'll need to be at a stage where it makes sense to say, well,

00:29:54.996 --> 00:29:59.815
let's start freezing more and more of the code base and let's just slow down?

00:29:59.815 --> 00:30:02.944
I think it depends on if there's any existential problems, right?

00:30:02.944 --> 00:30:06.550
When I talk to Sean about this, kind of the exact thing you're asking about,

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:11.876
he thinks quantum is the last major cryptographic problem, and he doesn't think any of

00:30:11.876 --> 00:30:15.480
the other ones are serious. But we can't predict, we can't—

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:16.425
Yeah, you don't know.

00:30:16.425 --> 00:30:20.929
So I think we kind of have to be just dynamic and adapting to it.

00:30:20.929 --> 00:30:24.107
Yeah. Obviously, as it becomes more and more important to the world,

00:30:24.107 --> 00:30:28.836
there will be more and more people aligned, right, to make those decisions and to adapt

00:30:28.836 --> 00:30:30.373
it to the future, right?

00:30:30.373 --> 00:30:35.800
You can definitely coordinate without things like ZCG, or even retroactive grants,

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:38.236
really. Yeah, that's a different discussion, right?

00:30:38.236 --> 00:30:41.413
I think you can kind of individually fund these things.

00:30:41.413 --> 00:30:45.116
But yeah, we need to adapt if there's another quantum-type threat,

00:30:45.116 --> 00:30:48.812
right, which I don't foresee, other people don't foresee, but who knows.

00:30:48.812 --> 00:30:51.965
Agreed, agreed. Yeah, thank you so much for your time.

00:30:51.965 --> 00:30:53.965
Yeah.
